text:
Looking at the diff:

```javascript
// before
return prototype === null || prototype === Object.prototype;
// after
return prototype !== null || prototype === Object.prototype;
```

The patched condition is `prototype !== null || prototype === Object.prototype`. Since the guard already returned early for `null`, non-objects, and arrays, this makes `isPlainObject` return `true` for **every** remaining object — `Map`, `Set`, `Date`, class instances, anything with a custom prototype — because for all of them `prototype !== null` is true. It also flips the `Object.create(null)` case from `true` to `false`.

The observable break is in the dumper: `mapTag.identify` is `isPlainObject`, so values that previously matched no tag (and threw `YAMLException: unacceptable kind of an object to dump`) are now silently serialised via `mapTag` (e.g. `dump(new Map([['a', 1]]))` produces `{}` because `Object.keys()` of a `Map` instance is empty; a class instance is dumped as a bare map). `realMapTag.identify` and `omapTag.addItem` are also affected. Loading paths are essentially unchanged, but the dump behaviour is a clear caller-visible regression.

